# Shell scripts keep LF endings even in a Windows checkout.
#
# scripts/bundle-plugin.sh is meant to be run from WSL on a Windows machine —
# it refuses to run under MSYS — and WSL's bash reads a CRLF `set -euo pipefail`
# as `pipefail\r`, an invalid option name. The scripts are also run verbatim by
# CI on Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The dispatcher template is copied verbatim into bundles as a POSIX sh script,
# where a CRLF shebang line is "bad interpreter" on Linux and macOS.
*.sh.in text eol=lf

# Generated footprint documents keep LF, because `plugin-footprint --out` writes
# LF on every platform and §6's freshness layer is `regenerate, then require no
# diff`. Without this, a Windows checkout gets CRLF, the tool rewrites LF, and
# every one of these files shows as modified in `git status` forever after —
# noise on exactly the files a reviewer is meant to read.
docs/footprints/*.json text eol=lf

# Task 1 spike contract: the recorded V2 shell event shape. Must stay LF so
# `jq` checks and freshness diffs behave identically on every platform.
opencode/rtk-mcp-opencode/tests/shell-shape.json text eol=lf

# OpenCode plugin sources: skills frontmatter, opencode.jsonc, and TS are all
# parsed by line-oriented checks (`jq`, wiring greps, frontmatter readers), so
# they keep LF on every platform like the footprint documents above.
opencode/**/*.ts text eol=lf
opencode/**/*.json text eol=lf
opencode/**/*.jsonc text eol=lf
opencode/**/*.md text eol=lf

# The canonical Ghidra skill is baked into binaries at compile time
# (`include_str!` in shared/ghidra-mcp/src/skill_asset.rs), so its working-copy
# bytes ARE the emitted bytes. Without this pin a Windows checkout feeds CRLF
# to the compiler while the LF-pinned plugin copies expect LF output, and the
# emit byte-identity tests fail on a correct tree. Same class as the `*.sh`
# pin above: build inputs must not depend on the checkout platform.
shared/ghidra-mcp/skill/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The three emit outputs must match `emit-skill` stdout byte for byte, so they
# carry the same pin as their source: without it a Windows checkout holds CRLF
# copies while the (now LF) emitter prints LF, failing the byte-identity tests
# on a correct tree. (The opencode copy is already covered by opencode/**/*.md.)
claude-code/re-ghidra-mcp-cc/skills/ghidra-re-driver/SKILL.md text eol=lf
qwen/re-ghidra-mcp-qwen/skills/ghidra-re-driver/SKILL.md text eol=lf
